26-07-14","paper_title":"Budget Outturn Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://moderngov.threerivers.gov.uk/documents/s18691/18691.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Capital programme slippage of \u00a310.995m on Local Authority Housing Fund properties pushed to 2026/27","lead":"Major capital delivery delays suggest project management challenges or implementation constraints and could affect the council's ability to deliver housing commitments and housing fund deadlines.","iso":"2026-07-14","paper_title":"Budget Outturn Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://moderngov.threerivers.gov.uk/documents/s18691/18691.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"Budget Outturn Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://moderngov.threerivers.gov.uk/documents/s18691/18691.pdf","headline":"Original budget revised downward by \u00a31.130m during the year due to inflation and cost pressures","detail":"The budget agreed in January 2025 was \u00a31.130m higher than the revised budget used for outturn comparison. The revision occurred during the year to reflect cost pressures from persistent inflation, pay awards, and utilities costs.","category":"demand","matters":"Mid-year budget revisions of this scale indicate the council's budget planning underestimated cost pressures and had to be corrected in-year, suggesting either optimistic assumptions or forecasting weakness.","figures":"\u00a31.130m budget reduction","quote":"The latest agreed budget was \u00a31.130m lower than the original budget agreed in January 2025, after allowing for carry forwards from 2024/25. During the year the budget was amended to reflect cost pressures primarily arising from the impact of persistently high inflation. This cost pressure included the agreed pay award and increased supplier costs for utilities.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"Budget Outturn Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://moderngov.threerivers.gov.uk/documents/s18691/18691.pdf","headline":"Pay award for 2026/27 identified as most significant budget risk with negotiations expected to be protracted","detail":"The council has no settled pay award assumption for 2026/27 and expects negotiations to be protracted with the final position remaining unknown well into the financial year. Updates will follow in September budget monitoring.","category":"people","matters":"Unbudgeted pay costs will force service cuts or further use of reserves. Pay is the largest controllable cost in local government and uncertainty creates real budget risk.","figures":"No financial impact quantified yet; update due September 2026","quote":"The most significant risk to the budget is the pay award for 2026/27. An update on the estimated financial impact in 2026/27 will be included in the Budget Monitoring report in September. It is likely that the pay negotiations will be protracted, and the final position will remain unknown well into the financial year.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"Budget Outturn Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://moderngov.threerivers.gov.uk/documents/s18691/18691.pdf","headline":"Capital programme slippage of \u00a310.995m on Local Authority Housing Fund properties pushed to 2026/27","detail":"The council is rephasing \u00a310.995m of capital budget for LAHF housing delivery (garage site development and property purchases) into 2026/27, representing 74% of the total \u00a314.831m capital programme rephasing. The reasons for delay are not detailed.","category":"capital","matters":"Major capital delivery delays suggest project management challenges or implementation constraints and could affect the council's ability to deliver housing commitments and housing fund deadlines.","figures":"\u00a310.995m rephasing against total capital rephasing of \u00a314.831m","quote":"The most significant variation relates to the budget related to the delivery of Local Authority Housing Fund (LAHF) properties. Rephasing of \u00a310.995m into 2026/27 is required in order to deliver the new housing as part of the LAHF delivery of the garage sites and purchase of additional properties.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"Budget Outturn Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://moderngov.threerivers.gov.uk/documents/s18691/18691.pdf","headline":"Disclaimed audit opinions in prior years have triggered increased external audit fees and remediation work","detail":"The council incurred higher external audit costs during 2025/26 due to 'build back work required as a result of disclaimed audit opinions in prior years.' Disclaimed audit opinions indicate the auditors could not form an opinion on the financial statements, signalling prior governance or accounting issues that required remediation.","category":"audit","matters":"Disclaimed opinions and post-audit remediation work indicate governance or financial reporting issues. A lender needs confidence in the reliability of a council's financial reporting and the quality of its controls.","figures":"Increased audit fees mentioned but amount not separately quantified in the outturn","quote":"A variance on Corporate Management Costs as a result of increased external audit fees arising from the build back work required as a result of disclaimed audit opinions in prior years.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-05-28","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Annual Governance Statement 2025/26","paper_url":"https://moderngov.threerivers.gov.uk/documents/s17970/17970.pdf","headline":"Local Government Reorganisation to unitary status in April 2028 creates staffing, service continuity and financial commitment risks","detail":"Hertfordshire is moving to unitary government in April 2028. This is identified as a significant governance issue bringing 'additional risks around staffing and day to day service operation in the period leading to and post reorganisation' and requiring 'additional consideration of long term commitments, contract and partnership arrangements'. The council is managing through communication, succession planning and partnership working, but the outcome is uncertain.","category":"governance","matters":"Local government reorganisations are high-risk periods with track records of service disruption, staff turnover, and unexpected financial pressures. A council preparing for absorption into a larger unit in two years faces uncertainty over future budget settlement, service scope, and officer roles. Lenders need confidence that the council can maintain financial stability through this transition.","figures":"April 2028 is the target date for reorganisation","quote":"Hertfordshire is currently expected to be part of the currently proposed round of Local Government Reorganisation to move to unitary government from April 2028. This will bring additional risks around staffing and day to day service operation in the period leading to and post reorganisation and require additional consideration of long term commitments, contract and partnership arrangements.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-14"},"name":"Three Rivers","findings_total":29,"news_drafts_total":3}}